@article{merged1231,
 abstract = {Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) offers new opportunities to
support creativity and problem-solving in design thinking education.
However, its integration with collaborative virtual tools to enhance
student engagement and higher-order thinking skills (HOTs) remains
underexplored. This study examines the impact of a GAI-enhanced
collaborative whiteboard (GAICW) in a DT course on occupational hygiene.
Using a quasi-experimental design, 65 students were assigned to either
an experimental group using GAICW or a comparison group using a
conventional platform. Data were gathered through pre- and post-tests
and an open-ended questionnaire. The experimental group exhibited
significantly higher engagement across behavioral, cognitive, and
emotional dimensions, with large effect sizes. They also outperformed
the comparison group in creativity and critical thinking, with medium
effect sizes. This study demonstrates how GAICW integration in design
thinking education enhances student engagement and HOTs. Findings
indicate that GAICW effectively scaffolds complex thinking processes,
particularly in early design phases, while transforming teaching
approaches by fostering more dynamic student participation.},
