@article{merged1319,
 abstract = {The rapid evolution of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) offers
new possibilities for personalized learning, interdisciplinary research,
and institutional innovation in higher education. Yet, its full impact
remains uncertain, raising critical questions about academic integrity,
equity, governance, and readiness. In this paper, I examine how GenAI is
reshaping teaching and research. Drawing on examples from kinesiology, I
illustrate opportunities such as differentiated instruction, improved
assessment, and enhanced inquiry. I also address the ethical,
infrastructural, and cultural challenges that come with the adoption of
GenAI. The decentralized nature of higher education is presented as both
an asset and a barrier to building agile, inclusive, and ethically
grounded strategies. Rather than reactive or tool-centric responses, I
call for a deliberate, values-driven approach centered on human
oversight, privacy, access, and inclusion. Responsible GenAI adoption, I
argue, is not merely a technical task but a strategic and moral
imperative for the future of higher education.},
