@article{merged1526,
 abstract = {Purpose- Generative artificial intelligence (GAI) has transformed how we
work and communicate; however, polarised opinions on it exist. This
study examined how users perceive the benefits of GAI in their everyday
lives and systematically categorised these perceived outcomes. The
influence of demographic factors on users' perceptions was assessed
across different life domains. Design/methodology/approach-Data were
collected through an online survey of 886 GAI users. The analysis
included exploratory factor analysis to categorise perceived benefits
and non-parametric tests to identify demographic differences.
Reliability was assessed using multiple coefficients. Findings- The
analysis identified four distinct categories of GAI benefits: education
and work, everyday activities, social relations and leisure time.
Education and work and everyday activities had the highest overall
benefit perceptions (>80\%). However, users reported experiencing these
benefits ``sometimes{''} more than ``often{''}, particularly in
professional contexts. Significant demographic variations were observed:
women perceived greater benefits in education and work, younger users
(under 34) reported more benefits across all domains compared to those
aged 45 and above, and higher education levels correlated positively
with most domains except everyday activities, for which an inverse
relationship was noted. Practical implications- The findings offer
valuable insights for GAI developers aiming to prioritise user-centred
design and for policymakers crafting inclusive regulatory frameworks
that address diverse user needs. Originality/value- This study provides
the first systematic categorisation of the benefits of GAI use in
everyday life, extending beyond traditional output-focused evaluations.
It uniquely applies De Certeau's concepts of ``strategies{''} and
``tactics{''} to understand how GAI benefits users across diverse life
domains.},
