@article{merged1717,
 abstract = {Those of us involved in higher education must address critical, emergent
questions related to access and disability to fully realize the promise
and potential of integrating generative AI into teaching and learning.
Reframing questions Tanya Titchkosky asked in her monograph on
disability in the university, in `The Question of Access: Disability,
Space, and Meaning', we will explore the following: Who will be
consulted in these conversations?What disability experiences will be
considered, prioritized, and ignored?Where is disability (not) located
within generative AI?When should considerations of disabled people be
made? Will we be included from the start, or considered ex post?By
problematizing current and future (non)-responses to disability and
access in the integration of generative AI into higher education, we are
actively calling attention to the gap between universities' ostensible
commitments to equitable access and the realities that
disproportionately affect those with disabilities. This article works to
bridge that gap in a way that does not simply note the lack of
disability inclusion, but also clearly lays out the issues educators
need to pay attention to in coming years as generative AI impacts
teaching and learning.},
