@article{merged1725,
 abstract = {Situated in higher education contexts, this qualitative case study
explores the perceived threat to personal agency among graduate students
when using Generative AI (GAI) in academic writing (AW). Drawing on nine
focus-group interviews with 53 Teaching English as Foreign Language
(TEFL) learners at the master's level, the research investigates how
TEFL graduates reflect on GAI impact towards their personal agency and
the strategies they employ to make good use of GAI in their AW
processes. With thematic analysis, the findings show that participants
expressed concerns about how GAI affect their decisiveness, writer
identity, self-accountability, and self-reflectiveness before, during,
and after writing their theses and articles for publications. These
identified threats align with Bandura's model of human agency,
indicating the interconnectedness of forethought, responsiveness, and
self-reflection throughout the stages of initial planning, high-order
drafting skills, and revision. Furthermore, as teachers, these declines
in personal agency could negatively influence their students'
competencies and outcomes in the long run. The study concludes with
participants' actionable strategies and discussions for using GAI
ethically and responsibly without undermining academic growth.},
