@article{merged1807,
 abstract = {As generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) becomes more influential
in higher education, the student body's capacity to use GenAI safely and
ethically, particularly where misinformation is easily propagated, such
as nutrition, is still under-researched. In the present study, a
faculty-designed online nutrition course required students to engage in
AI-generated information over two semesters. With an enrollment of 424
students, researchers tracked student perspectives on the tool's
usability and trustworthiness. Results suggest most students are
initially more trusting of information created by the AI than that of
other sources, despite beginning the course with mixed opinions about
GenAI's usefulness. As the semester progressed, students became more
scrutinizing of AI-generated information, despite a significantly
greater number of students also confessing a positive opinion of the
tool's place in higher education. With this change, students found the
platform easier to use, with a heightened desire to continue using it.},
