@article{merged1881,
 abstract = {This study explores the use of generative AI, exemplified by ChatGPT,
for self-directed professional development (SDPD) among K-12
schoolteachers. Specifically, it examines how ChatGPT assists teachers
in their instructional practices and teaching methods, supports their
SDPD, and explores teachers' perceptions of its role in this process.
Based on Garrison's (Garrison, Adult Education Quarterly 48:18-33, 1997)
self-directed learning framework, we utilized a mixed-method approach to
collect and analyze 298 completed survey responses, five individual
semi-structured interviews, and documents (e.g., ChatGPT chat history
regarding SDPD usage). Our research found that teachers primarily used
ChatGPT to get inspiration and structure for classroom pedagogies, class
activities, and lesson plans. Notably, while schoolteachers' motivation
for SDPD was generally passive due to teaching pressure; ChatGPT, in
fact, played an important role in motivating teachers to advance their
SDPD. Teachers demonstrated high self-management and self-monitoring
capabilities in using ChatGPT for self-directed professional development
(SDPD), including the ability to apply a variety of learning strategies
and track time and goals while using ChatGPT for SDPD. In addition,
teachers generally felt confident when interacting with ChatGPT for SDPD
purposes. The findings of this research suggest strategies for teachers
to enhance their teaching performance through the integration of
ChatGPT. These findings may also be beneficial to policymakers and
educators seeking to understand the potential of ChatGPT in supporting
SDPD and inform future explorations of its role in informal learning
contexts.},
